thanks for asking that in the in the  forum here  um it you know in order for uh a  off-target effect in order for a a um  a guide to to have an off-target effect  it would have to be an off-target  binding to another regulatory region  so that really shrinks the the space for  for off targeting  um we have definitely performed rna-seq  on our on our data sets to compare the  specificity  of our repressor um to the crab  repressor  and we've found that that there's just  really very very minimal  um bystander effects resulting from  from using these guys um we did look at  the pooling of guide rnas versus  individuals  as well and really didn't see any  increase  in in differentially expressed uh  transcripts so  uh so uh yes i mean we definitely offer  the pools and individual reagents so you  can follow up on  correlation and address any off-target  concerns uh the other thing this is  that's come up  a bit is whether there's any you know  truly non-specific actions  as far as you know acting on on next  door neighbor jeans or across the street  neighbor jeans  um and we just really because of that  limited window for efficacy where these  guides need to be placed in relation to  the transcription start site  we really um don't see any uh any  uh evidence of these effects so it's a  reasonable concern and something we're  certainly keeping our eye on so um yeah  i think the beauty of the  the system is the specificity in large  part and you have to have kind of  prolonged contact for that  crispr mediated crispr immediate  repression so um yeah it's cool thanks  for the thanks for the question  of course so moving on to our next  question you mentioned the time course  for safely and transfected cells  how long do you expect the repression  mediated by dcas9  cell 1 sds3 mrna to persist for uh  yeah right yeah thanks i not have  mentioned that in passing  so we see really robust transcriptional  repression that comes  on very very quickly as you're giving it  kind of a large bolus of  reagents and then this uh we recommend  this approach for those  short endpoint assays in the two to four  day range  uh i'd say is the is the safe um  window to be looking at with when using  the the mrna  along with the synthetic single guides  and continuing on with um repression  what level of repression should i expect  to see with crispr i  and how does the level compare to fi rna  effects  yeah sure so you know this is um  the level of transcriptional repression  with crispr eye is gonna  you know like dealing with these pesky  biological systems right so you're going  to have some variants depending on your  gene target  cell line time point of analysis etc  uh we definitely see you know comes  online quickly the  the target gene repression is maximal in  the 48 to 96 hour uh sort of window and  we we really  we've seen that 50 to 80 and i called it  about 60 to 80 percent knocked down  uh during that time um  so yeah so so this is this is  definitely somewhat less than we  observed with our si rna reagents  we expect those usually in the 80 to  sort of 95  knock down area great  now our next question is it possible to  use crispr and crisper eye together  for activating a gene and inactivating  another one  at the same time wow  cool  yeah so kind of looking at simultaneous  activation and  um and interference there's some some  technical hurdles  um there as far as the the guide design  and  you know controlling which genes to turn  on and which means to turn off when  mixing them all  so um so at this point no and at this  point we're not able to do simultaneous  uh  switch on switch off across across  various gene targets  but it's a super cool idea and something  certainly i'm sure us and others are are  keen on  on uh and seeing if there's a way to  accomplish that because that would be  would be super cool  and so our next question steve do you  have  the crispr mod crisper eye as an  inducible system  right we don't we don't have an  inducible uh  flavor of this at this time  great thank you so we do want to just  remind our audience you know we've  received so many great questions in  today  um any questions that we don't have time  for or unable to get to today  they'll be answered by steve um or a  member of horizon discovery  via the email address that you  registered for this webinar with  so steve let's move on to our next  question  how are the synthetic guides or  synthetic  excuse me how are the synthetic guides  or synthetic guides plus  dcas9 cell 1 sds3  introduced into the cells is it for  example nucleophile  yeah so um yeah thanks i might not have  mentioned that well  in passing but we've done both lipid  transfections  and nuclear infections with mrna plus  the  the single guides so whatever your cells  would uh would tolerate the best will  will work  wonderful thank you now so for the  multiplexing of many sgrna against  different targets  was that done with a single sg rna gene  or  the pools of three  yeah um good question thanks so this is  um  so it was when going to the multiplexing  we dialed it into the best performing  individual guide  so we suggest starting with the pooled  guide approach and coming in  and um and starting with those pools and  um  and then experimenting with them  individually  and dialing in the best performing uh  individual component  and then using that as part of the the  um  the multiplexed pool approach  great thank you so our next question  does the crisper eye guide  share the same sequence of the horizon  crispr a off the shelf  products oh right yeah good question  um thanks for that they're they're not  so we have to they have to nest  on on sort of opposite sides of the  transcription start site  so the the crispr a guides um you know  are just  upstream and the crisper eye guides uh  sit just just downstream of that to kind  of have the  the right uh actions uh on transcription  so um yeah so they're they're close  they're their 